# Everything here is OPTIONAL. Boxes runs with no .env at all:
#
#   docker compose up -d        ->  http://localhost:3000
#
# Copy this file to .env only to change something. Whatever it contains
# reaches the orchestrator as-is; compose.yaml names nothing in it at all. The
# values shown commented out are the defaults, and they live in
# orchestrator/src/config.ts, which parses and validates all of this at boot.
#
# No credential is set here. The Claude token, the OpenAI key a Codex thread
# runs on, the GitHub token and the git identity are entered on the
# dashboard's settings page and kept in the data volume, because a credential
# has to be enterable without a restart and a login has no static form at all.
# See README, "The settings page".

# ---- where the port is published ----
# Loopback by default, because the orchestrator has no authentication of its
# own. Change this only when something else is providing that auth: a reverse
# proxy on this host already reaches 127.0.0.1, and one in a container should
# join the `boxes_default` network and use `orchestrator:3000` instead.
#BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1
#HOST_PORT=3000

# ---- egress ----
# Hosts sessions may reach, comma or whitespace separated. Exact names and
# one-label wildcards (`*.example.com` matches `a.example.com`, not
# `a.b.example.com` and not `example.com`). Unset is the default: any public
# host, with private ranges still denied.
#
# The hosts of a credential the settings page holds are always reachable, so a
# narrow list can never cut a session off from inference or from GitHub. That
# covers api.openai.com for a Codex thread as well, and the hosts Codex logs
# in and refreshes at (auth.openai.com) and the endpoint a ChatGPT
# subscription talks to (chatgpt.com), which come with the OpenAI credential
# without ever being sent it.
#
# Two more hosts Codex uses are nobody's implication and a deployment's own
# choice: files.openai.com, which model attachments are fetched from, and
# ab.chatgpt.com, its telemetry. Under a list that omits them the proxy
# refuses both -- expect them in its denial counters -- and Codex carries on.
#EGRESS_ALLOWED_HOSTS=github.com,*.github.com,*.githubusercontent.com,api.anthropic.com,api.openai.com,registry.npmjs.org

# ---- sessions ----
#SESSION_IMAGE=ghcr.io/splitbrain/boxes/session:latest
# A session cannot apt-get install anything -- read-only rootfs, non-root, no
# capabilities. It equips itself out of /home/agent instead (npm's prefix and
# pipx point there, ~/.local/bin is on PATH), and a package the whole
# deployment should have goes in an image built FROM the published one, named
# here.
#
# Who a session runs as, and so who owns every file in a workspace. 1020
# rather than 1000, which is the base image's own uid and usually a person's
# on a real host. The session image has to be built on the same numbers --
# it takes them as AGENT_UID and AGENT_GID build args -- because a session's
# home is a named volume Docker initialises from the image, and no one can
# chown that afterwards. Setting these to the uid the orchestrator itself
# runs as is what lets it drop root.
#SESSION_UID=1020
#SESSION_GID=1020
# How often the session image is pulled again, so a tag like :latest moves
# here too. A running session is never disturbed; it adopts what arrived the
# next time it is started. 0 turns the refresh off, which is what an image
# built on this host wants -- there is no registry to pull it from. A missing
# image is still pulled once either way, because nothing can be created
# without it.
#SESSION_IMAGE_PULL_MINUTES=60
# Whether the copy of the session image a pull replaced is removed from this
# host. It is a gigabyte or two per release otherwise, untagged and kept
# forever -- nothing else is going to reclaim it. Only images carrying the
# session image's own label are touched, never one this host holds for some
# other reason, and never one a container is still on: the daemon refuses
# those, and the session moves off at its next start. Turn it off for a host
# that keeps old images to roll back to.
#SESSION_IMAGE_PRUNE=true
#SESSION_SUBNET_POOL=10.200.0.0/16
#SESSION_MEM_LIMIT=4g
#SESSION_CPUS=2
#SESSION_PIDS_LIMIT=512
#IDLE_STOP_MINUTES=30

# How often a box is asked what work -- a command left running, a monitor
# watching something -- is still running in it, in seconds. A Docker API call
# per running session per window, and it is also how a thread's own list of
# what it left running goes away when the work ends: nothing reports that, so
# the reading is the only news there is. Polled on this clock while a browser
# is attached; asked when the reaper sweeps otherwise.
#BACKGROUND_POLL_SECONDS=20

# How long a thread has to say nothing before the agent counts as having
# stopped, and how long it then has to stay quiet before anybody is told the
# turn is over. The first is a fallback: the adapter Boxes ships with marks
# the end of a cycle itself, and only an adapter that says nothing needs a
# gap measured. The second always applies -- the screen can correct itself,
# and a push notification cannot.
#AGENT_QUIET_SECONDS=3
#AGENT_SETTLE_SECONDS=30

# Largest single file a prompt may carry into a workspace, in MiB. Attachments
# are written to .boxes/attachments/ in the session's own workspace.
#MAX_ATTACHMENT_MB=25

# ---- permissions ----
# hold | deny -- what to do with a permission request nobody answered in time.
#PERMISSION_FALLBACK=hold
#PERMISSION_HOLD_MINUTES=120

# ---- notifications ----
# Web Push needs no configuration: the deployment generates a VAPID keypair
# into DATA_DIR the first time a browser subscribes. This is only the contact
# a push service uses when it has a problem with what the deployment sends it;
# a mailto: or an https: URL, and nothing else.
#PUSH_SUBJECT=https://github.com/splitbrain/boxes

# ---- logging ----
# Lowest severity written to stderr: debug, info, warn or error.
#
# `debug` carries every ACP message forwarded in either direction, which is
# the one switch that shows what an agent and the adapter actually said to
# each other. It is a lot of output for a busy deployment, so the default is
# one step above it.
#LOG_LEVEL=info

# ---- storage ----
# A session's workspace is a directory under DATA_DIR, bind-mounted into its
# container, so the orchestrator can read the agent's files without an exec.
# The bind source is resolved by the Docker daemon, so it must be named with
# the path the *daemon* knows DATA_DIR by; the orchestrator works that out at
# boot by inspecting its own container, which with the shipped compose gives
# /var/lib/docker/volumes/boxes-data/_data.
#
# Set this only where that cannot work: a nested or rootless daemon, or a
# compose file that mounts a real host directory for /data.
#HOST_DATA_DIR=
